module

public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.FiniteProducts
public import Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.Opposite.Subcategory
public import StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.DerivedPushforward.MixedInternalHomPullbackIso
public import StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.DerivedPushforward.ProjectionFormulaStrictlyPerfect

/-!
# A strictly perfect complex is generated by the structure sheaf

Let `Y` be a ringed space and let `P` be a strictly perfect complex of `𝒪_Y`-modules, that is a
bounded complex each of whose terms is a direct summand of a finite free `𝒪_Y`-module
([Stacks, Tag 08C4]).  This file proves that `P` lies in the strictly full saturated triangulated
subcategory of `D(𝒪_Y)` generated by the structure sheaf placed in degree zero, in the form
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedInternalHomPullback.MixedTranspose.GeneratedByOp` — the membership
predicate read in `D(𝒪_Y)ᵒᵖ`, which is the category in which the first argument of an internal
hom varies.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedInternalHomPullback.generatedByOp_of_isStrictlyPerfect`: the
  statement above.

## Why this is wanted

`MixedTranspose.GeneratedByOp` is the hypothesis `hK` of
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedInternalHomPullback.isIso_derivedPullbackSheafHomMap_of_generatedByOp`
and of `MixedTranspose.isIso_homComparison_of_generatedByOp`, and it had **no producer anywhere**:
every occurrence of it was its own definition, a docstring, or a hypothesis binder.  So the two
dévissage theorems that consume it could not be applied to any object other than the generator
itself.  This supplies the producer at the complexes [Stacks, Tag 0GM7] clause (1) is about.

It is *not* clause (1) of Tag 0GM7, and it carries no attribute.  Two things still stand between
the two: the dévissage theorems also carry shift data — a `CategoryTheory.Functor.CommShift` and
a `CategoryTheory.Functor.IsTriangulated` for each side of the comparison, in the
**contravariant** variable of `Stacks.SheafOfModules.derivedSheafHom`, where no `CommShift`
exists in this development; and perfectness in the sense of [Stacks, Tag 08CM] is a *local*
condition, so a perfect object is only locally quasi-isomorphic to a strictly perfect complex,
which is the source's first step "the question is local on `Y`".

## Implementation notes

The argument is the one of
`Stacks.Cohomology.StrictlyPerfectInvertibility.isIso_app_of_isStrictlyPerfect` with the property
"a natural transformation is invertible here" replaced by an arbitrary `ObjectProperty`, and it is
run through `Stacks.DerivedCategory.BoundedInduction.property_of_bounded_complex` in the same way.
Three differences are worth recording.

*The property lives on the opposite category, and is transported rather than restated.*
`GeneratedByOp` quantifies over `Pr : ObjectProperty D(𝒪_Y)ᵒᵖ`, while the induction is over
complexes and so wants a property of `D(𝒪_Y)`.  The transport is Mathlib's
`CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.unop`, for which `Pr.unop.IsTriangulated` and
`Pr.unop.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms` are already instances
(`Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.Opposite.Subcategory` and
`Mathlib.CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.Opposite`).  Only stability under retracts has to be
moved by hand, and it is immediate: a retract `i ≫ r = 𝟙` in `D(𝒪_Y)` is the retract
`r.op ≫ i.op = 𝟙` in the opposite category.

*No shifts are quantified over.*  The template carries its property at every shift, because
invertibility of a fixed natural transformation is not a priori shift-stable and its extension
step moves the vertices of a triangle.  Here shift-stability is a *field* of
`ObjectProperty.IsTriangulated`, so the plain property suffices and the induction is run at it.

*The biproduct step is Mathlib's, not this repository's.*  An `ObjectProperty` which is
triangulated and closed under isomorphisms is closed under finite products — the instances
`ObjectProperty.IsClosedUnderBinaryProducts` and `ObjectProperty.IsClosedUnderFiniteProducts` of
`Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Triangulated.Subcategory`, which get them from the split distinguished
triangle `binaryProductTriangle_distinguished`.  So the finite free module needs no induction on
its rank: `finiteFree Y m` is by definition the biproduct of `m` copies of the structure module,
`DerivedCategory.singleFunctor` is additive and therefore preserves it, and
`CategoryTheory.Limits.biproduct.isoProduct` turns the biproduct into the product the
`ObjectProperty` API speaks about.

*The two spellings of the structure sheaf of rings.*  As in
`StacksFrontier.Topology.Sheaves.Cohomology.DerivedPushforward.ProjectionFormulaStrictlyPerfect`,
`Stacks.SheafOfModules.Tensor.ringSheaf Y.sheaf` — in which
`Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedProjectionFormula.singleUnit` and the category
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSpace.KFlatDerivedPullback.Modules` are stated — and
`StrictlyPerfect.structureSheafOfRings Y` — in which the strictly perfect vocabulary of Tag 08C4
is — are verbatim copies of each other, but both are semireducible, so a goal mixing them is not
type-correct at `implicit` transparency.  Both are made reducible at that transparency by
`attribute [local implicit_reducible]`.

*The transports of the property are seated by name at the retract step.*  Transparency at
`implicit` is not transparency at `instances`.  `Pr`, bound by `MixedTranspose.GeneratedByOp`, is
a property of the objects of `D(𝒪_Y)ᵒᵖ` read at the `Tensor.ringSheaf` spelling, because that is
the spelling of the generator `singleUnit Y` it is applied to, while
`prop_singleFunctor_finiteFree_of_prop_singleUnit` asks for `Pr.unop` at the
`StrictlyPerfect.OXMod` one, because that is where `finiteFree` and `structureModule` live.
Mathlib's transports then do not fire: instance search unifies at `instances` transparency, where
neither sheaf of rings unfolds, so a transport proved at `Pr`'s own index is invisible at the
other.  The two instances it needs,
`IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms` and `IsTriangulated`, are therefore seated by name at
`ObjectProperty.unop (C := DerivedCategory (OXMod Y)) Pr` — the index written out rather than
inferred — and the application is written at that same spelling.  The `letI`s hold the transports
Mathlib already proves, read at the other index; nothing is assumed and no instance is declared.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 08C4](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08C4)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 08CM](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08CM)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0ATI](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ATI)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GM7](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GM7)
-/

@[expose] public noncomputable section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits CategoryTheory.Pretriangulated
open TopologicalSpace Opposite
open StrictlyPerfect
open Stacks.DerivedCategory.BoundedInduction

universe u

namespace Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedInternalHomPullback

attribute [local instance] HasDerivedCategory.standard
attribute [local implicit_reducible] structureSheafRing
  StrictlyPerfect.structureSheafOfRings CategoryTheory.sheafCompose
attribute [local instance_reducible] AlgebraicGeometry.SheafedSpace.sheaf

variable {Y : RingedSpace.{u, u}}
  [HasSheafify (Opens.grothendieckTopology Y.carrier) AddCommGrpCat.{u}]
  [(Opens.grothendieckTopology Y.carrier).WEqualsLocallyBijective AddCommGrpCat.{u}]

/-- **Stability under retracts transports to the opposite category.**  A retract in `C` is a
retract in `Cᵒᵖ` with the two morphisms exchanged, so an `ObjectProperty` of `Cᵒᵖ` stable under
retracts has a retract-stable `CategoryTheory.ObjectProperty.unop`.

Mathlib carries the corresponding transports for `IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms`,
`IsStableUnderShift` and `IsTriangulated` but not this one. -/
theorem ObjectProperty.isStableUnderRetracts_unop {C : Type*} [Category C]
    (Pr : ObjectProperty Cᵒᵖ) [Pr.IsStableUnderRetracts] :
    Pr.unop.IsStableUnderRetracts where
  of_retract {A B} r hB :=
    Pr.prop_of_retract
      (X := op A) (Y := op B)
      { i := r.r.op
        r := r.i.op
        retract := by rw [← op_comp, r.retract, op_id] }
      hB

/-- **The structure sheaf placed in a degree lies in the subcategory generated by the structure
sheaf placed in degree zero.**  This is `singleUnitDegreeIso` together with the shift-stability
of a triangulated `ObjectProperty`. -/
theorem prop_singleFunctor_structureModule_of_prop_singleUnit
    (Pr : ObjectProperty (DerivedCategory (OXMod.{u, u} Y)))
    [Pr.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms] [Pr.IsTriangulated]
    (hU : Pr ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (OXMod.{u, u} Y) 0).obj (structureModule Y)))
    (i : ℤ) :
    Pr ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (OXMod.{u, u} Y) i).obj (structureModule Y)) :=
  Pr.prop_of_iso (ProjectionFormulaPerfect.singleUnitDegreeIso (Y := Y) i)
    (Pr.le_shift (-i) _ hU)

/-- **A finite free module placed in a degree lies in the subcategory generated by the structure
sheaf.**  `finiteFree Y m` is the biproduct of `m` copies of the structure module,
`DerivedCategory.singleFunctor` preserves it, and a triangulated `ObjectProperty` closed under
isomorphisms is closed under finite products. -/
theorem prop_singleFunctor_finiteFree_of_prop_singleUnit
    (Pr : ObjectProperty (DerivedCategory (OXMod.{u, u} Y)))
    [Pr.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms] [Pr.IsTriangulated]
    (hU : Pr ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (OXMod.{u, u} Y) 0).obj (structureModule Y)))
    (i : ℤ) (m : ℕ) :
    Pr ((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (OXMod.{u, u} Y) i).obj (finiteFree Y m)) := by
  have hterm := prop_singleFunctor_structureModule_of_prop_singleUnit Pr hU i
  refine Pr.prop_of_iso
    (((DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (OXMod.{u, u} Y) i).mapBiproduct
        (fun _ : Fin m => structureModule Y)).trans
      (biproduct.isoProduct _)).symm ?_
  exact Pr.prop_product (fun _ => hterm)

/-- **A strictly perfect complex lies in the thick triangulated subcategory generated by the
structure sheaf.**  For a ringed space `Y` and a strictly perfect complex `P` of `𝒪_Y`-modules,
every property of the objects of `D(𝒪_Y)ᵒᵖ` which is closed under isomorphisms and retracts, is
triangulated, and holds at the structure sheaf placed in degree zero holds at `P`.

This is the producer `MixedTranspose.GeneratedByOp` did not have.  It carries no `@[stacks]`
attribute: it is one step of the dévissage of [Stacks, Tag 0GM7] clause (1), not that clause —
see the module docstring for the two things that still separate them. -/
theorem generatedByOp_of_isStrictlyPerfect
    (P : CochainComplex (OXMod.{u, u} Y) ℤ) (hP : IsStrictlyPerfect P) :
    MixedTranspose.GeneratedByOp
      (Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedProjectionFormula.singleUnit Y) (DerivedCategory.Q.obj P) := by
  intro Pr hiso hret htri hU
  let := hiso
  let := hret
  let := htri
  let := ObjectProperty.isStableUnderRetracts_unop Pr
  refine property_of_bounded_complex Pr.unop ?_ ?_ ?_ P hP.bounded ?_
  · exact fun e h => Pr.unop.prop_of_iso e h
  · exact fun _ hA => Pr.unop.prop_of_isZero hA
  · intro S hS h₁ h₃
    exact Pr.unop.ext_of_isTriangulatedClosed₂ _
      (DerivedCategory.triangleOfSES_distinguished hS) h₁ h₃
  · intro i
    obtain ⟨m, ⟨r⟩⟩ :=
      (isDirectSummandOfFiniteFree_iff_retract (P.X i)).mp (hP.directSummand i)
    let : ObjectProperty.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms
        (ObjectProperty.unop (C := DerivedCategory (OXMod.{u, u} Y)) Pr) :=
      inferInstanceAs Pr.unop.IsClosedUnderIsomorphisms
    let : ObjectProperty.IsTriangulated
        (ObjectProperty.unop (C := DerivedCategory (OXMod.{u, u} Y)) Pr) :=
      inferInstanceAs Pr.unop.IsTriangulated
    exact Pr.unop.prop_of_retract
      (r.map (DerivedCategory.singleFunctor (OXMod.{u, u} Y) i))
      (prop_singleFunctor_finiteFree_of_prop_singleUnit
        (ObjectProperty.unop (C := DerivedCategory (OXMod.{u, u} Y)) Pr) hU i m)

end Stacks.Cohomology.DerivedInternalHomPullback

end
